================================================================================
port/02-image-container/mtdsplit_cvimg.port
Rootfs split decision for the RX2L Pro on the 6.18 / latest-OpenWrt base.
Chosen option: (a) REBASE our mtdsplit_cvimg parser. Not fixed-partitions.
================================================================================

0. THE DECISION IN ONE PARAGRAPH
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Keep the cvimg split parser. It is three small overlay files on top of an
OpenWrt framework that verifiably still exists on 6.18, the parse callback
signature is byte-for-byte identical to the 4.14 one, and it buys the one
property this port cannot get any other way: our factory image has the *same
on-flash shape as a genuine Tenda KernelFS* (rootfs at exactly 16+len, nothing
in between), so every install path the vendor's own code controls -- stage-1
boot, the `cr6c`/`r6cr`-aware AUTOBURN/TFTP burn parser, `mtd write` of the
container, `tendaupgrade` -- sees a layout it already accepts. fixed-partitions
requires us to invent a padded kernel region, i.e. a shape no Tenda image has,
and to pin the rootfs offset blindly before we know what a 6.18 kernel plus the
rtl8192cd HAL actually compresses to. Cost of the choice: one out-of-tree
parser (~250 lines, already written and already audited on this hardware) plus
two lines in the mtdsplit Kconfig/Makefile.


1. WHAT THE FRAMEWORK LOOKS LIKE ON 6.18 (verified against master today)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Nothing here is assumed; all four were read out of openwrt/openwrt@master on
2026-11-07:

* `target/linux/generic/files/drivers/mtd/mtdsplit/` still exists and holds 24
  files (mtdsplit.c, mtdsplit.h and 22 parsers). There is NO mtdsplit_cvimg.c
  upstream -- it disappeared with the glinet boards, so this parser is ours.
* The hook is `target/linux/generic/pending-6.18/400-mtd-mtdsplit-support.patch`
  (8141 bytes). Its changes to *upstream kernel source* are exactly:
    - drivers/mtd/Kconfig:              new menu "OpenWrt specific MTD options"
                                        adding MTD_ROOTFS_ROOT_DEV (default y),
                                        MTD_SPLIT_FIRMWARE (default y),
                                        MTD_SPLIT_FIRMWARE_NAME ("firmware"),
                                        and `source "drivers/mtd/mtdsplit/Kconfig"`.
    - drivers/mtd/Makefile:             `obj-$(CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT) += mtdsplit/`
    - drivers/mtd/mtdpart.c:            includes <linux/magic.h> and
                                        "mtdsplit/mtdsplit.h"; moves the
                                        part_parser list/spinlock under the
                                        patch, adds
                                        parse_mtd_partitions_by_type(),
                                        run_parsers_by_type(), split_firmware(),
                                        mtd_partition_split(), calls
                                        mtd_partition_split() from
                                        add_mtd_partitions() and from
                                        mtd_add_partition(), and deletes the
                                        now-duplicated static helpers.
    - include/linux/mtd/mtd.h:          mtd_roundup_to_eb(),
                                        mtd_rounddown_to_eb(),
                                        mtdpart_get_offset().
    - include/linux/mtd/partitions.h:   `enum mtd_parser_type` and the new
                                        `.type` member of struct
                                        mtd_part_parser.
    - hack-6.18/420-mtd-support-OpenWrt-s-MTD_ROOTFS_ROOT_DEV.patch: in
                                        add_mtd_device(), sets ROOT_DEV for a
                                        partition whose name is "rootfs".
  => the parser-selection rule the DTS must respect is unchanged from 4.14:
     a DT partition named `firmware` *without* a `compatible` property gets the
     MTD_PARSER_TYPE_FIRMWARE parsers run over it, and children created by
     mtd_add_partition() are split recursively. Adding
     `compatible = "..."` to our firmware node would silently disable the whole
     mechanism -- same trap the toby base documents in rtl8197f_tenda_ac23.dts.
* The callback prototype in 6.18 is still
      int (*parse_fn)(struct mtd_info *, const struct mtd_partition **,
                      struct mtd_part_parser_data *);
  (partitions.h, untouched by the patch apart from `.type`), and mtdsplit.h
  still exports mtd_check_rootfs_magic()/mtd_find_rootfs_from()/
  mtd_get_squashfs_len() with the same arguments. So the 4.14 body of our
  parser compiles as-is.
* `register_mtd_parser()` still exists in mainline partitions.h
  (__register_mtd_parser + macro), so `subsys_initcall` registration is still
  legal; master's own parsers have moved to `module_mtd_part_parser()`.


2. THE FILE SET (3 overlay files + 2 config lines)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
files/target/linux/generic/files/drivers/mtd/mtdsplit/
  mtdsplit_cvimg.c     NEW, section 3 below (the whole file, rebased)
  Makefile           PATCHED, add one line to master's version
  Kconfig            PATCHED, add one stanza to master's version

⚠ Do NOT carry our 4.14 copies of Makefile/Kconfig over the new base. Those are
whole-file overlays of a 19.07-era list; copying them onto master silently
deletes eight parser entries that other targets in the tree still use
(BCM63XX, CFE_BOOTFS, SEIL, ELF, H3C_VFS, MSTC_BOOT, OPENWRT_PROLOG, …) and the
resulting kernel just fails to build those, or worse, an unrelated target loses
its split. Patch master's files instead:

--- a/target/linux/generic/files/drivers/mtd/mtdsplit/Makefile
+++ b/target/linux/generic/files/drivers/mtd/mtdsplit/Makefile
@@ -18,3 +18,4 @@ obj-$(CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT_ELF_FW) += mtdsplit_elf.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT_H3C_VFS) += mtdsplit_h3c_vfs.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT_MSTC_BOOT) += mtdsplit_mstc_boot.o
 obj-$(CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT_OPENWRT_PROLOG) += mtdsplit_owrt_prolog.o
+obj-$(CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT_CVIMG_FW) += mtdsplit_cvimg.o

--- a/target/linux/generic/files/drivers/mtd/mtdsplit/Kconfig
+++ b/target/linux/generic/files/drivers/mtd/mtdsplit/Kconfig
@@ -119,3 +119,11 @@ config MTD_SPLIT_OPENWRT_PROLOG
 	bool "OpenWrt executable prolog-based parser"
 	depends on MTD_SPLIT_SUPPORT
 	select MTD_SPLIT
+
+config MTD_SPLIT_CVIMG_FW
+	bool "Realtek cvimg (cr6c/cs6c) based firmware partition parser"
+	depends on MTD_SPLIT_SUPPORT
+	select MTD_SPLIT
+	help
+	  Splits a Realtek cvimg "firmware" partition into kernel + rootfs using
+	  the IMG_HEADER_T len field: the rootfs starts at exactly 16 + len.

Target kernel config (rtl8197f/config-6.18, alongside the existing
CONFIG_MTD_ROOTFS_ROOT_DEV / CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT_FIRMWARE which the 400-patch
defaults to y):

  CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT_CVIMG_FW=y
  CONFIG_MTD_ROOTFS_ROOT_DEV=y
  CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT_FIRMWARE=y
  # CONFIG_MTD_SPLIT_SQUASHFS_ROOT is not set
      ^ deliberately OFF, same as the primary base: the rootfs partition stays
        "everything from 16+len to the end of firmware", and mount_root carves
        rootfs_data out of its tail. Enabling the squashfs root parser would
        trim the partition to squashfs bytes_used and leave the overlay area as
        an unlabelled hole that mount_root does not own.

DTS: partition@40000 label "firmware", reg <0x40000 0xf80000>, NO compatible
property (RX2LPRO.dts already satisfies all three; keep it that way).


3. THE REBASED PARSER
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Delta from files/target/linux/generic/files/drivers/mtd/mtdsplit/
mtdsplit_cvimg.c (4.14 version, dated 2026-11-07) is small and each hunk is
numbered here:

  D1  kzalloc -> kcalloc                            (master style, no behaviour
                                                    change)
  D2  header sanity: check retlen, and bound
      16 + len <= master->size instead of only
      len <= master->size                            (a len that passes the old
                                                    check can still point the
                                                    read past the end)
  D3  rootfs_offset >= master->size rejection      (off-by-one: an offset equal
                                                    to the size yields a
                                                    zero-length rootfs)
  D4  keep subsys_initcall() + register_mtd_parser()
      instead of master's module_mtd_part_parser().
      Reason, concrete to this board: mtd_partition_split() only sees parsers
      that are already on the list, and the partition walk happens when the
      SPI-NOR device is added, i.e. from the sheipa-spi / spi-nor probe.
      module_driver()/builtin_driver() register at device_initcall (level 6),
      the same level the platform drivers register at, so the ordering becomes
      link-order-dependent. subsys_initcall (level 4) is strictly earlier than
      any of those probes, which is what every OpenWrt mtdsplit parser did
      before the macro conversion and what this target needs. This is the ONE
      behavioural difference from master's parsers and it is deliberate.
  D5  no changes to the offset logic. The 2026-11-07 fix (try 16+len exact
      first, then ALIGN(16+len,4), then the 0xa0000/4 KiB scan) is the part
      that makes this board boot: cvimg's len is payload+2 and the payload is
      only 2-byte aligned, so on this container the superblock sits at an
      offset like 0x3c0812, which ALIGN(...,4) walks straight past and which a
      4 KiB-step scan can never reach. Keep it verbatim.

/*
 *  Copyright (C) 2013 Gabor Juhos <juhosg@openwrt.org>
 *  Copyright (C) 2017 Weijie Gao <hackpascal@gmail.com>
 *  Copyright (C) 2026 rx2l-pro-openwrt contributors (cvimg split for RTL8197F)
 *
 *  This program is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify it
 *  under the terms of the GNU General Public License version 2 as published
 *  by the Free Software Foundation.
 *
 *  Splits a Realtek cvimg ("cr6c"/"cs6c") firmware partition into
 *  kernel + rootfs. The rootfs offset comes from the 16-byte IMG_HEADER_T
 *  len field rather than from a magic scan, because len is what the vendor
 *  stage-1 uses as its copy length and cvimg puts the squashfs superblock
 *  at exactly 16 + len. Verified byte-exactly against this board's own
 *  KernelFS dump and two decrypted vendor images.
 */

#include <linux/module.h>
#include <linux/init.h>
#include <linux/kernel.h>
#include <linux/slab.h>
#include <linux/sizes.h>
#include <linux/mtd/mtd.h>
#include <linux/mtd/partitions.h>
#include <linux/byteorder/generic.h>

#include "mtdsplit.h"

#define CVIMG_SEARCH_LIMIT	SZ_2M
#define CVIMG_MIN_ROOTFS_OFFSET	0xa0000
#define CVIMG_ROOTFS_ALIGNMENT	SZ_4K

#define SIGNATURE_LEN		4

#define FAKE_ROOTFS_IDENT	"FAKE"
#define FAKE_ROOTFS_IDENT_SIZE	4
#define FAKE_ROOTFS_IDENT_OFS	4
#define FAKE_ROOTFS_SIZE_OFS	16

#define CVIMG_NR_PARTS		2

enum data_type {
	DATA_UNKNOWN,
	DATA_KERNEL,
	DATA_KERNEL_WITH_ROOTFS
};

struct signature {
	enum data_type	type;
	const char	*sig;
};

/*
 * cr6c is what this board's stage-1 accepts and what a cvimg "firmware"
 * payload emits; cs6c is the kernel-only sibling (also accepted by the
 * loader). cr6b/cs6b/csys/csro are the older cvimg.c signatures, kept so the
 * parser still recognises a sibling container -- note that OUR loader
 * refuses cr6b/cs6b outright, so listing them here is parser tolerance, not
 * bootability.
 */
const static struct signature sig_known[] = {
	{ .type = DATA_KERNEL,		.sig = "cs6b" },
	{ .type = DATA_KERNEL,		.sig = "cs6c" },
	{ .type = DATA_KERNEL,		.sig = "csys" },
	{ .type = DATA_KERNEL_WITH_ROOTFS, .sig = "cr6b" },
	{ .type = DATA_KERNEL_WITH_ROOTFS, .sig = "cr6c" },
	{ .type = DATA_KERNEL_WITH_ROOTFS, .sig = "csro" },
};

struct img_header {
	unsigned char signature[SIGNATURE_LEN];
	unsigned int start_addr;
	unsigned int burn_addr;
	unsigned int len;
};

static enum data_type signature_type(const char *sig)
{
	int i;

	for (i = 0; i < ARRAY_SIZE(sig_known); i++)
		if (!memcmp(sig, sig_known[i].sig, SIGNATURE_LEN))
			return sig_known[i].type;

	return DATA_UNKNOWN;
}

static int mtd_find_cvimg_rootfs_from(struct mtd_info *mtd,
				      size_t from,
				      size_t limit,
				      size_t *ret_offset,
				      enum mtdsplit_part_type *type)
{
	size_t offset;
	int err;

	for (offset = from; offset < limit;
	     offset = ALIGN(offset + CVIMG_ROOTFS_ALIGNMENT,
			    CVIMG_ROOTFS_ALIGNMENT)) {
		err = mtd_check_rootfs_magic(mtd, offset, type);
		if (err)
			continue;

		*ret_offset = offset;
		return 0;
	}

	return -ENODEV;
}

static int mtdsplit_parse_cvimg(struct mtd_info *master,
				const struct mtd_partition **pparts,
				struct mtd_part_parser_data *data)
{
	struct img_header hdr;
	size_t hdr_len, retlen;
	size_t temp_offset, rootfs_offset, aligned;
	struct mtd_partition *parts;
	char ident[FAKE_ROOTFS_IDENT_SIZE];
	u32 fake_rootfs_size;
	int err;

	hdr_len = sizeof(hdr);
	err = mtd_read(master, 0, hdr_len, &retlen, (void *)&hdr);
	if (err)
		return err;
	if (retlen != hdr_len)				/* D2 */
		return -EIO;

	/* D2: the field is what the stage-1 copy length is derived from, so a
	 * header that names bytes past the partition is always corrupt. */
	if (be32_to_cpu(hdr.len) > master->size - sizeof(hdr))
		return -EINVAL;

	/*
	 * The old single candidate was ALIGN(sizeof(hdr) + hdr.len, 4).
	 * That is wrong for the Tenda RX2L Pro container and can silently miss
	 * our own images too: cvimg writes len = payload_size + 2 with the
	 * payload aligned to 2 bytes only, and puts the 2-byte checksum
	 * immediately after the payload, so the rootfs starts at exactly
	 * sizeof(hdr) + len -- NOT on a 4-byte boundary. Measured on this
	 * board's KernelFS: len = 0x3c0802, superblock at 0x3c0812, and
	 * ALIGN(0x3c0812, 4) = 0x3c0814 is two bytes past it. Whether an image
	 * hits the aligned candidate then depends on the kernel size modulo 4,
	 * i.e. the split would work one build and not the next; the 4 KiB-step
	 * scan fallback cannot rescue it either, because an offset like
	 * 0x3c0812 is never 4 KiB aligned.
	 *
	 * So: exact offset first, then the 4-aligned candidate for containers
	 * that pad their payload, then the scan.
	 */
	temp_offset = sizeof(hdr) + be32_to_cpu(hdr.len);

	switch (signature_type(hdr.signature)) {
	case DATA_KERNEL:
	case DATA_KERNEL_WITH_ROOTFS:
		err = mtd_check_rootfs_magic(master, temp_offset, NULL);
		if (!err) {
			rootfs_offset = temp_offset;
			break;
		}

		/* containers that pad the payload to 4 bytes instead of 2 */
		aligned = ALIGN(temp_offset, sizeof(uint32_t));
		if (aligned != temp_offset &&
		    !mtd_check_rootfs_magic(master, aligned, NULL)) {
			rootfs_offset = aligned;
			break;
		}

		err = mtd_find_cvimg_rootfs_from(master, CVIMG_MIN_ROOTFS_OFFSET,
						 master->size, &rootfs_offset,
						 NULL);
		if (err)
			return err;

		err = mtd_read(master, rootfs_offset + FAKE_ROOTFS_IDENT_OFS,
			       FAKE_ROOTFS_IDENT_SIZE, &retlen, ident);
		if (err)
			return err;
		if (retlen != FAKE_ROOTFS_IDENT_SIZE)
			return -EIO;

		if (!memcmp(ident, FAKE_ROOTFS_IDENT, FAKE_ROOTFS_IDENT_SIZE)) {
			err = mtd_read(master, rootfs_offset + FAKE_ROOTFS_SIZE_OFS,
				       sizeof(fake_rootfs_size), &retlen,
				       (void *)&fake_rootfs_size);
			if (err)
				return err;

			fake_rootfs_size = ntohl(fake_rootfs_size);

			err = mtd_check_rootfs_magic(master,
					rootfs_offset + fake_rootfs_size, NULL);
			if (!err) {
				rootfs_offset += fake_rootfs_size;
				break;
			}

			err = mtd_find_cvimg_rootfs_from(master,
				ALIGN(rootfs_offset + fake_rootfs_size,
				      CVIMG_ROOTFS_ALIGNMENT),
				master->size, &rootfs_offset, NULL);
			if (err)
				return err;
		}

		break;
	default:
		return -EINVAL;
	}

	if (rootfs_offset >= master->size)		/* D3 */
		return -EINVAL;

	parts = kcalloc(CVIMG_NR_PARTS, sizeof(*parts), GFP_KERNEL);	/* D1 */
	if (!parts)
		return -ENOMEM;

	parts[0].name = KERNEL_PART_NAME;
	parts[0].offset = 0;
	parts[0].size = rootfs_offset;

	parts[1].name = ROOTFS_PART_NAME;
	parts[1].offset = rootfs_offset;
	parts[1].size = master->size - rootfs_offset;

	*pparts = parts;
	return CVIMG_NR_PARTS;
}

static struct mtd_part_parser mtdsplit_cvimg_parser = {
	.owner		= THIS_MODULE,
	.name		= "cvimg-fw",
	.parse_fn	= mtdsplit_parse_cvimg,
	.type		= MTD_PARSER_TYPE_FIRMWARE,
};

/* D4: subsys_initcall, not module_mtd_part_parser -- see the delta list. */
static int __init mtdsplit_cvimg_init(void)
{
	register_mtd_parser(&mtdsplit_cvimg_parser);
	return 0;
}

subsys_initcall(mtdsplit_cvimg_init);

/* Keep sig_known[] in sync with files/tools/firmware-utils/src/cvimg.c's own
 * sig_known[] table: the writer and the parser must agree on which 4-byte
 * strings mean "kernel + rootfs in this container".
 */


4. WHY NOT fixed-partitions (what would break, and what it would cost)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
rx23302 can use fixed-partitions because its flash has *separate* kernel and
rootfs partitions at fixed offsets and its vendor loader wants the rootfs at a
fixed address anyway. On this board:

a) mtd3 is ONE partition (0x40000..0xfc0000) holding container+rootfs+overlay,
   and the genuine stock shape puts the superblock at 16+len = 0x3c0812 -- an
   offset only a parser can know. fixed-partitions would force us to pad the
   kernel region to a chosen erase-aligned constant and then ship a container
   with padding after its checksum. The loader tolerates that (it never reads
   past `len`), but the file stops looking like any Tenda image: the vendor's
   AUTOBURN/burn parser keys "Find sqhs" off the container, and the shape we
   would be betting on is untested.
b) The padding choice is a blind bet on a kernel we have not built yet. 6.18 +
   rtl8192cd + rtl8197f-eth + flow offload LZMA'd lands somewhere in
   3.5-5 MiB; pick 0x400000 and one config change over 4 MiB bricks every
   image; pick 0x600000 and we permanently lose 2 MiB of a 15.5 MiB window to
   filler. mtdsplit has no constant to revisit.
c) sysupgrade: with a fixed split, writing the whole firmware partition is
   wrong unless the padding is exact, so platform_do_upgrade has to split the
   writes (rx23302 does: erase kernel, write rootfs, write kernel last). That
   also throws away the property that sysupgrade.bin == factory-cr6c.bin byte
   for byte, i.e. one more image to keep in sync and one more way to flash a
   half-written set. Under mtdsplit, default_do_upgrade's single
   `get_image | mtd write - firmware` is correct by construction.
d) root device: unchanged either way -- hack-6.18/420 makes the partition
   *named* "rootfs" the ROOT_DEV, and mtdsplit creates that name. The 4.14 port
   already depends on it (there is no `root=` in the DT bootargs); the risk
   that dependency documents (VFS: Unable to mount root fs on
   unknown-block(0,0)) is the same for both options, so it is not a tie-breaker.
e) Upstream-purity: fixed-partitions avoids a kernel patch. That is real, but
   the patch is not ours to maintain alone -- it sits on a framework OpenWrt
   keeps alive for ath79/ramips/bmips/bcm47xx, and the 6.18 callback signature
   is identical to 4.14's, so the maintenance surface is one file whose
   interface has been stable for a decade.

If the rebase is ever rejected (e.g. the split stops appearing in /proc/mtd
because of an SPI-NOR/OF-node change), the fallback is fully specified: pad
`append-kernel | rx2lpro-pad-to-KERNELPART` to a fixed 0x500000, declare
kernel/rootfs/rootfs_data partitions at 0x40000/0x540000/…, replace
platform_do_upgrade with the rx23302 three-step write, and teach
tools/check-cr6c.py the fixed rootfs offset instead of the derived one.


5. HOW TO PROVE IT ON THE ROUTER (no writes involved)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
First boot through RAM (initramfs image, loader `XMOD` + `J:`) or after a
flashed image, over serial only:

  cat /proc/mtd
      -> must show, inside "firmware":
         mtdN "kernel"  size 0x<len+16>
         mtdN "rootfs"  size 0xf80000-<that>
  dmesg | grep -i 'cvimg\|partitions found on MTD'
      -> "2 cvimg-fw partitions found on MTD device firmware"
     If it says "unrecognised partition table" or only "firmware" exists, the
     parser never registered (D4) or the firmware node acquired a `compatible`
     property (section 1).
  ls /dev/mtdblock*; mount | grep squashfs; cat /proc/cmdline | tr ' ' '\n' | grep root=
      -> root= must be MTDBLOCK for the "rootfs" partition; that is
         CONFIG_MTD_ROOTFS_ROOT_DEV firing on the name, i.e. risk #2 of
         README's first-boot checklist.
